# Activity 2: Color Palette & Typography Set

## Description
This activity focused on creating a cohesive visual identity board featuring a three-color palette—**Red (`#d1412e`)**, **Light Orange (`#f6f4f0`)**, and **Light Red (`#efebeb`)**—paired with three complementary typefaces (**Vintage Rotter**, **Baksoda**, and **Trocchi**)[cite: 3].

## What Have I Learned?
- **Key Concepts & Skills:** I gained an understanding of how hex color codes, tonal contrast, and font pairings (combining expressive script/display fonts with a clean serif typeface) establish a consistent mood and readability across a project[cite: 3].
- **Challenge & Solution:** Balancing light background tones (`#f6f4f0` and `#efebeb`) so they remained distinguishable while keeping text crisp was tricky; I solved this by outlining the light swatch cleanly and using `#d1412e` as a high-contrast primary accent color[cite: 3].
- **Future Application & Improvement:** I can use structured style boards like this before designing websites, presentations, or nursing education handouts, and I could improve it by specifying exact usage roles (e.g., primary heading, subheading, body text, and background) for each font and color.
